<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_files-streams-and-persistent-data">
    <title>Files, Streams, and Persistent Data</title>

    <p>
        A program usually receives input and produces output through streams.
        Standard input and output connect a program to the keyboard and screen;
        file streams connect a program to files stored on a device.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table_chapter12_stream-endpoints">
        <title>Common C++ stream endpoints</title>
        <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
            <col width="25%"/><col width="20%"/><col width="55%"/>
            <row header="yes">
                <cell>Stream</cell>
                <cell>Direction</cell>
                <cell>Typical endpoint</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>std::cin</c></cell>
                <cell>Input</cell>
                <cell>Keyboard</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>std::cout</c></cell>
                <cell>Output</cell>
                <cell>Screen</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>std::ifstream</c></cell>
                <cell>Input</cell>
                <cell>File</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>std::ofstream</c></cell>
                <cell>Output</cell>
                <cell>File</cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
        A file is persistent data: it can remain available after a program
        finishes. Persistence allows a program to save records, reports, and
        configuration so that a later run can use them. In software engineering,
        separating persistent data from program code also makes data easier to
        inspect, back up, exchange, and test.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_stream-operations">
        <title>Streams move data</title>
        <p>
            The insertion operator <c>&lt;&lt;</c> sends formatted data into an
            output stream. The extraction operator <c>&gt;&gt;</c> obtains
            formatted data from an input stream. <c>std::getline()</c> obtains
            a complete line, including spaces that ordinary extraction would
            stop before.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_stream-operations"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int quantity{};
    std::cin >> quantity;

    std::cout << "Quantity: " << quantity << '\n';
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_text-and-binary-preview">
        <title>Text and binary persistence</title>
        <p>
            A text file stores characters arranged according to a documented
            format. A binary file stores bytes according to a documented binary
            format. Text is convenient for people to inspect, while binary data
            can represent fixed-width values compactly and support direct access.
            Neither format is automatically self-describing; the program needs
            to know how to interpret the stored data.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter12_1_stream-endpoints">
        <statement><p>Which stream normally represents input from a file?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::cout</c></p></statement><feedback><p><c>std::cout</c> is normally connected to screen output.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>std::ifstream</c></p></statement><feedback><p>An input file stream reads data from a file.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::ofstream</c></p></statement><feedback><p>An output file stream writes data to a file.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::cin</c></p></statement><feedback><p><c>std::cin</c> is normally connected to keyboard input.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>std::string</c></p></statement><feedback><p>A string stores characters but is not itself a file stream.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_1_persistence">
        <statement><p>Select all benefits of persistent data.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It can be used by a later run of the program.</p></statement><feedback><p>Files remain after the program ends.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It can be backed up or exchanged separately from the program.</p></statement><feedback><p>Persistent files are separate artifacts.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It guarantees that a file format never needs documentation.</p></statement><feedback><p>Programs still need rules for interpreting stored data.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It makes every input operation succeed.</p></statement><feedback><p>Files can be missing, corrupted, or inaccessible.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It replaces the need for program memory.</p></statement><feedback><p>Programs still load and process data in memory.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_1_stream-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to read a quantity and report it through the standard streams.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>int quantity{};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>std::cin &gt;&gt; quantity;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; "Quantity: " &lt;&lt; quantity &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
